\description{
Read protein databases (UniProt XML or FASTA) into one row per protein, with GO terms and Ensembl gene links as long tables on request.

Loads each database with mzLib's \code{ProteinDbLoader}, exactly as a search would but with no decoys
generated, and answers "what is this accession?": organism, NCBI taxon, gene names, length and
mass, plus - when you ask for them - its Gene Ontology terms and Ensembl gene links as long
tables.
}
\details{
\strong{A FASTA knows no GO terms and no Ensembl genes.} Its headers carry organism (\code{OS=}), taxon
(\code{OX=}) and gene name (\code{GN=}) and nothing else, so a FASTA contributes no \code{go_terms} or
\code{ensembl_genes} rows. That is the format's silence, not a biological absence, and \code{files} says
so in its \code{absent_fields} column. Read the UniProt XML of the same proteome for those.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_protein_database}. \code{file_count}, \code{read_count} and \code{failed_count} count
  database files; \code{record_count} counts the proteins that passed the accession filter;
  \code{accession_filter_count} counts the distinct accessions in the filter, and is \code{NA} with no
  filter; \code{accessions_not_found} is \code{NULL} with no filter. \code{tables} names what was returned,
  \code{caveats} the traps in the result as a whole, and \code{files} has one row per input (its
  \code{source_index}, \code{contaminant}, \code{protein_count}, \code{decoy_count}, \code{record_count}, \code{caveats},
  \code{absent_fields} and any \code{error_*}).

  \code{proteins} is a data.frame with one row per protein, or \code{NULL} when it was not requested:
  \code{length} in residues and \code{monoisotopic_mass} in Da (of the unmodified sequence as written,
  plus one water; \code{NA} when the sequence holds a letter with no defined mass). \code{gene_names} and
  \code{ensembl_gene_ids} are list columns. \code{go_terms} and \code{ensembl_genes} are data.frames, or \code{NULL}
  when not requested. **\code{source_index} is 1-based** in all of them, so
  \code{db$files[db$proteins$source_index, ]} finds each row's database.

}
\section{Masses are of the precursor as written}{


\code{monoisotopic_mass} is the unmodified sequence plus one water - the initiator methionine, signal
peptide and propeptide included - so it is not the mass of the mature protein.

}
\section{No decoys, no variants}{


A database is read as written. A decoy already in the file (an accession starting \code{DECOY}) is
kept and flagged \code{is_decoy}; none are generated. A UniProt XML that records a \strong{genotype} still
has those variants applied by mzLib, which renames the accession (\code{P38936_C117Y}); the file's
\code{caveats} say how many.

}
